{"name":"Dickie Bird","hex":"#60b8be","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dickie bird","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of dicky-bird."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dickie_bird"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dickie Bird","blocks":[{"text":"Harold Dennis \"Dickie\" Bird was an English cricketer and international cricket umpire. During his long umpiring career, he became a well regarded figure among players and the viewing public, not only due to his high standards as an umpire but also for humour and eccentricity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Dickie_Bird.JPG/330px-Dickie_Bird.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dickie_Bird"},{"title":"Bird","blocks":[{"text":"Birds are vertebrate animals constituting the class Aves, characterised by feathers, toothless beaked jaws, the laying of hard-shelled eggs, a high metabolic rate, a four-chambered heart, and a strong yet lightweight skeleton. They are warm-blooded, live worldwide, and range in size from the 5.5 cm (2.2 in) bee hummingbird to the 2.8 m common ostrich. There are over 11,000 living species of birds, classified in 44 orders. More than half of the world's bird species are passerine or \"perching\" birds. Nearly all birds have wings whose development varies according to the life style of each species. Wings, which are modified forelimbs, gave birds the ability to fly, although further evolution has led to the loss of flight in some birds, including ratites, penguins, and diverse endemic island species. The only known groups without wings are the extinct moa and elephant birds. Birds are the only extant members of Dinosauria, and are a type of paravian theropods. The digestive and respiratory systems of birds are uniquely adapted for flight. Some bird species of aquatic environments, particularly seabirds and some waterbirds, have further evolved for swimming. The study of birds is called ornithology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Kleiber_Flug.jpg/330px-Kleiber_Flug.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bird"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Dickie_Bird_-_geograph.org.uk_-_257723.jpg/500px-Dickie_Bird_-_geograph.org.uk_-_257723.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Dickie_Bird_-_geograph.org.uk_-_257723.jpg/500px-Dickie_Bird_-_geograph.org.uk_-_257723.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dickie Bird umpiring the Fourth Test between England and New Zealand in 1983 at Trent Bridge, standing between Ian Botham and Richard Hadlee","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADickie_Bird_-_geograph.org.uk_-_257723.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/The_Statue_of_a_Renowned_Cricket_Umpire.JPG/500px-The_Statue_of_a_Renowned_Cricket_Umpire.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/The_Statue_of_a_Renowned_Cricket_Umpire.JPG/1280px-The_Statue_of_a_Renowned_Cricket_Umpire.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/The_Statue_of_a_Renowned_Cricket_Umpire.JPG/500px-The_Statue_of_a_Renowned_Cricket_Umpire.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Statue of Bird as an umpire, in Barnsley, in 2009","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Statue_of_a_Renowned_Cricket_Umpire.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Archaeopteryx_lithographica_%28Berlin_specimen%29.jpg/500px-Archaeopteryx_lithographica_%28Berlin_s